{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786942197,"nonce":"JHlFSDJqq1Ip4MFFSG7LwQfW0Mo=","title":"**What is wrong.** `createSource` is declared `Promise\u003cSource\u003e` at `web/src/api/endpoints.ts:463-465` and calls `postItem\u003cSource\u003e`, but the handler answers `SourceCreatedDTO` — `{source, ingest_token, token_prefix, webhook_url}` (`api/openapi/openapi.yaml:7569-7593`, `internal/sources/api/sources.go:200-208`), which is a different object with a `SourceDTO` nested inside it. The single call site compensates with a cast through `unknown`: `props.onCreated(created as unknown as SourceCreated)` at `web/src/features/settings/SourcesSection.tsx:542`. Two consequences follow. The generated contract types cannot check this path at all — `as unknown as` is precisely the cast that suspends them — and `webhook_url`, which the response carries and the operator needs, is never rendered: `TokenDialog` (`SourcesSection.tsx:759-781`) shows `ingest_token` and a copy button, and tells the reader to \"Point your Alertmanager's webhook receiver at oto's ingest URL for this source\" without giving the URL it was just handed.","message":"**The demonstration.** The description of `webhook_url` in the contract is \"The absolute URL to paste into `webhook_config.url`\" (`api/openapi/openapi.yaml:7589-7593`), and `deploy/alertmanager/alertmanager.yml`'s header comment sends the operator to it: \"`webhook_url` from that response goes in `url`, `ingest_token` goes in `credentials`\". An operator who registers a source through the UI receives half of that pair on screen and has to reconstruct the other half — `{base}/api/v1/ingest/alertmanager/{id}` — from documentation, using an id the dialog also does not show. The value is in the response body the dialog is rendering.\n\n**Why it matters.** Small in isolation, and it is the seam the other two source tickets land on: rotate answers the same `SourceCreatedDTO` (`internal/sources/api/sources.go:389-394`), so a return type that is already wrong would be wrong in a second place the moment a rotate wrapper is added, and the `as unknown as` at the call site would be copied with it. The missing URL is also the difference between the dialog being a token display and being the handoff the register flow exists to complete — `webhook_url` is server-built from the deployment's own `OTO_HTTP_BASE_URL` (`.env.example:9`), so it is the one version of that URL nobody has to assemble.\n\n**Where.** `web/src/api/endpoints.ts:463-465` (`createSource`'s return type); `web/src/features/settings/SourcesSection.tsx:539-545` (the mutation and its cast), `:740-790` (`TokenDialog`); `web/src/api/types.ts:115` (`SourceCreated`, already declared and already correct); `api/openapi/openapi.yaml:7569-7593`; `internal/sources/api/sources.go:200-208`.\n\n**Scope.** small.\n\n**Done when.** `createSource` returns `Promise\u003cSourceCreated\u003e`, the cast at the call site is gone, and no `as unknown as` remains on that path. The one-time dialog shows the webhook URL beside the token, each copyable on its own, so what the operator carries to `alertmanager.yml` is both halves of the pair the file's own header comment asks for. `webhook_url` is optional in the contract, so the dialog renders it when present rather than assuming it.","files":null}]}